 # Migration tool: convert the twelve existing sandpiper boards to Trail layout
 
-Trail ships neutral `.trail/config.json` defaults and no longer understands the `.sandpiper` layout. Twelve boards on this machine are still in the old shape. They need a one-time conversion.
+Standalone Trail reads `.trail/config.json` and knows nothing about the `.sandpiper` layout. The boards on this machine are all in the old shape. A separate tool converts them.
 
-**This lives in sandpiper-ai, not Trail.** That is the whole point of the split: sandpiper-ai already knows the old formats because it wrote them, so the migration can read them without Trail ever learning a legacy dialect it would then carry forever.
+## Settled design
 
-Scope:
-- Move `.sandpiper/tasks/` to `.trail/tasks/` and write a `.trail/config.json` in the strict total shape TCL-149 now enforces. Hand-written configs are the norm here — 11 of the 12 boards have one — so the converter must handle partial/loose input and emit the total form, not assume defaults will be filled in.
-- Preserve separate-branch and external-repo storage settings, including worktree/clone state, rather than re-bootstrapping.
-- Idempotent, and safe to abort: these are live boards with real history.
+`.sandpiper/docs/specs/trail-extraction-design.md`, "Migration tool" — marked **[settled]**.
 
-**Absorb the toon 4.x index rewrite here (TCL-156).** Trail now writes `index.toon` with toon 4.1.1, whose keyed tabular form 2.3.x cannot read. Since this tool rewrites indexes anyway, the format change should ride along with it instead of becoming a second migration event — that was the reasoning for taking 4.1.1 over the 2.3.1 patch. The ordering constraint in TCL-156 still applies: every consumer must be on 4.x before any board is rewritten.
+A separate, **throwaway** CLI. It exists because we are currently Trail's only consumers; it is our migration, not a Trail feature.
 
-Verification must assert resulting on-disk and VCS state, not exit codes and banners. Three bugs found during the extraction (TCL-151, TCL-152, TCL-153) were all commands reporting intent instead of result, and all were invisible to tests that checked stdout.
+**It does not live in the Trail repo.** Keeping it in sandpiper-ai is what lets it read `.sandpiper-tasks.json` and `.sandpiper/settings.json` — Sandpiper's formats — without Trail ever knowing they existed. That ownership boundary is the whole reason Trail gets to be compat-free.
 
-Deferred out of TCL-146; filed 2026-08-12 when that ticket reached review.
+**Interface:** `--tasks-dir`, plus an optional `--config-file`. It emits `./.trail/config.json` with the tasks-dir key pointed at the existing `./.sandpiper/tasks`.
 
----
+**Tasks are not moved.** The point is to make old directories work in place, which keeps the migration reversible and decouples "adopt standalone Trail" from "reorganize every repo."
 
-## Survey of the actual boards, 2026-08-12
+It must carry the whole `version_control` block forward, not just the tasks dir.
 
-Eleven boards found under `~/git`. The shape is not what "migrate the config" suggests.
+**Depends on TCL-163.** The tasks-dir config key does not exist yet; without it there is nothing to point at the old location with.
 
